	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Action">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">action</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, &apos;Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,&apos; Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2026, pp. 35930–35948. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Basic Formal Ontology (BFO), &apos;process&apos; (BFO:0000015). http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000015</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">Basic Formal Ontology (BFO), &quot;process&quot; (BFO:0000015). http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000015</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>a goal-directed physical operation carried out by an actor involving one or more physical entities, whose feasibility is evaluated using explicitly represented feature descriptions and requirements</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific terminology definition based on the Action hierarchy of the conceptual model. The concept is general, but the definition is formulated for consistency with the project&apos;s action and feasibility representation rather than adopted verbatim from a source. Added during conceptual model revision. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from BFO&apos;s concept of a process involving physical entities. The ontology represents Action as a physical action type whose feasibility is evaluated through explicitly represented physical features and action requirements. This is a project-specific abstraction and is not declared equivalent to BFO Process. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Cutting, opening, grasping, and moving may be represented as actions when modeled at an appropriate level.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use Action to represent the evaluated action, and Assessment to connect a candidate, target, and action through hasCandidate, hasTarget, and hasAction. Do not infer feasibility from an action name or an object&apos;s conventional function.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Action</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action is an action-general ontology class. The current implementation focuses on CUT, represented as an individual of Action. The ontology evaluates action feasibility through explicitly represented physical features and action requirements.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">AFFORDANCE20Q describes affordance reasoning in terms of actions supported by physical properties, and RIO describes affordances as functions or potential actions offered by objects.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">action requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">represented physical condition relevant to determining whether an action is feasible</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Implementation provenance: this term/label is represented in the project&apos;s internal research pipeline (step5_requirement_induction.py). These internal files are retained for project traceability and are not used as bibliographic sources. Definition adaptation note: Project-specific working definition synthesized from the use-case specification and provisional requirement representation; not a verbatim definition from either source. Lexical basis only: General meaning of a requirement; the action-specific physical condition is defined in the ontology. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT example: sufficient rigidity of a working edge to maintain its geometry under applied force.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Used by the reasoning system to compare known physical properties and configurations with the represented requirements of a specified action in order to support a feasibility judgment.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Action Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-general concept. The current course implementation instantiates Action Requirements for CUT, but requirements may be represented for other physical actions in future extensions.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">An Action Requirement may involve one physical property, a combination of properties, or a physical configuration. Multiple requirements may be jointly necessary within one modeled pathway, while alternative requirement configurations may support the same action. Represented requirements are modeling claims and should not automatically be interpreted as universally necessary or sufficient physical laws.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Assessment">
		<rdfs:subClassOf>
			<owl:Restriction>
				<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasAction"/>
				<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Action"/>
				<owl:qualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:qualifiedCardinality>
			</owl:Restriction>
		</rdfs:subClassOf>
		<rdfs:subClassOf>
			<owl:Restriction>
				<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasJudgment"/>
				<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeasibilityJudgment"/>
				<owl:qualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:qualifiedCardinality>
			</owl:Restriction>
		</rdfs:subClassOf>
		<rdfs:subClassOf>
			<owl:Restriction>
				<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasCandidate"/>
				<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalEntity"/>
				<owl:qualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:qualifiedCardinality>
			</owl:Restriction>
		</rdfs:subClassOf>
		<rdfs:subClassOf>
			<owl:Restriction>
				<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasTarget"/>
				<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalEntity"/>
				<owl:qualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:qualifiedCardinality>
			</owl:Restriction>
		</rdfs:subClassOf>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">assessment</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:source>
		<skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Feasibility Assessment</skos:altLabel>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">evaluation context that relates a candidate physical entity, a target physical entity, and an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>The implemented RDF/OWL ontology uses “Assessment” as the class label. “Feasibility Assessment” is retained as an alternate label from the earlier conceptual-model terminology. Lexical basis only: General meaning of evaluation; candidate–target–action connections are local modeling decisions. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin. Alternate label was locally assigned; no external source is claimed.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">testAAssessment links testCandidateA, carrotTarget, and the CUT action for evaluation.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use the Assessment class for entities satisfying its intended role and modeled restrictions; supply relevant assertions explicitly.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Assessment</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to evaluation contexts that organize candidate, target, and action information for physical-feasibility reasoning.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">The assessment is a particular candidate–target–action context. Its outcome depends on explicitly represented candidate and target physical features and requirement mappings, not the candidate’s lexical name or conventional function.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ContactAreaFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/GeometricFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">contact area feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” Findings of ACL: EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">geometric feature describing the area of a physical entity available for contact during an interaction</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific extension of local geometry and part-scoped descriptions. The paper does not define contact area or contact mechanics. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): This source supports treating physical characteristics as evidence for affordance reasoning. Team Affordance defines ContactAreaFeature locally as: &quot;geometric feature describing the area of a physical entity available for contact during an interaction&quot;. The publication is conceptual motivation, not a formal OWL class definition. Dictionary entry supplies only the ordinary meaning of the physical characteristic. AFFORDANCE20Q is retained as background motivation, not as the source of this exact OWL class definition.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A supplied candidate description identifies a narrow working contact region.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Attach the known property to its actual physical bearer; use it only through evidence-backed requirement relationships.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Contact Area Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-independent property category. Known candidate-side values are supplied as input; object labels are not reasoning evidence.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Contact area may depend on configuration and loading. Missing contact-region information must remain unresolved.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ContactAreaRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/GeometricRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">contact area requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” Findings of ACL: EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">geometric requirement concerning the contact area needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific specialization motivated by physical interaction and part-level descriptions. The paper does not define a contact requirement. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Parent aligned with Diagram 1. Requirement class created by Team Affordance to specify a condition over a modeled feature; AFFORDANCE20Q motivates physical-property evidence but does not define this OWL class. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A modeled CUT pathway requires contact at the candidate’s designated working region.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Compare supplied facts with the represented requirement. Explicit conflict and missing information are distinct outcomes.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Contact Area Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-general requirement category. Its specific constraints depend on the action and represented physical conditions. Target-conditioned required levels may be expressed through RequirementMapping when explicitly modeled; no fixed threshold is implied by this class alone.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">A contact requirement concerns an interaction. Contact Area Property describes the geometry of its contact region.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/DescribedObject">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">described object</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:contributor xml:lang="en">LZ</dcterms:contributor>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:source>
		<owl:equivalentClass>
			<owl:Class>
				<owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
					<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/WholeObject">
					</rdf:Description>
					<owl:Class>
						<owl:unionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
							<owl:Restriction>
								<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasFeatureValue"/>
								<owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureValue"/>
							</owl:Restriction>
							<owl:Restriction>
								<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasPart"/>
								<owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ObjectPart"/>
							</owl:Restriction>
						</owl:unionOf>
					</owl:Class>
				</owl:intersectionOf>
			</owl:Class>
		</owl:equivalentClass>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">object represented by at least one explicit object part or physical feature value</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific OWL defined class. Membership is grounded in explicitly represented object parts or physical feature values rather than an object&apos;s lexical name. Lexical basis only: Supports describing an entity by features; the DescribedObject OWL equivalence and restrictions are local. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative DescribedObject case: a test object or assessment meeting the relevant class restrictions.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class when the stated structural or assessment restrictions are supported by explicitly modeled assertions.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Described Object</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Includes whole objects with at least one represented part or physical feature value; membership alone does not establish CUT feasibility.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">DescribedObject is a locally defined structural or reasoning classification; check its OWL axioms before generalizing from observed test cases.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/EdgeGeometryFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/GeometricFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">edge geometry feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” Findings of ACL: EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Shape.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/shape</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Shape.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/shape</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">geometric feature describing the shape or form of an edge relevant to physical interaction</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Adapted from sharp-edge examples and part-scoped SHAPE/SIZE predicates. The property category is project-specific. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): This source supports treating physical characteristics as evidence for affordance reasoning. Team Affordance defines EdgeGeometryFeature locally as: &quot;geometric feature describing the shape or form of an edge relevant to physical interaction&quot;. The publication is conceptual motivation, not a formal OWL class definition. Dictionary entry supplies only the ordinary meaning of the physical characteristic. AFFORDANCE20Q is retained as background motivation, not as the source of this exact OWL class definition.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">The supplied working region has a thin or sharp edge geometry.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Attach the known property to its actual physical bearer; use it only through evidence-backed requirement relationships.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Edge Geometry Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-independent property category. Known candidate-side values are supplied as input; object labels are not reasoning evidence.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Assign local edge geometry to its bearer rather than to every part of the object.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/EdgeGeometryRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/GeometricRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">edge geometry requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">geometric requirement concerning the edge geometry needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate edge geometry value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning edge geometry; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Edge Geometry Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to action requirements concerning edge geometry relevant to physical interaction.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">EdgeGeometryRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeasibilityJudgment">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">feasibility judgment</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:source>
		<owl:equivalentClass>
			<owl:Class>
				<owl:unionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
					<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Feasible">
					</rdf:Description>
					<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Infeasible">
					</rdf:Description>
					<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/InsufficientInformation">
					</rdf:Description>
				</owl:unionOf>
			</owl:Class>
		</owl:equivalentClass>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">judgment representing the outcome of evaluating whether a candidate can physically support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class representing feasibility judgments, with Feasible, Infeasible, and InsufficientInformation as subclasses; distinct from named judgment individuals used in CUT test cases Lexical basis only: General meaning of an evaluation; specific judgment categories and criteria are defined locally. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">feasibleJudgment and insufficientInformationJudgment are named judgment individuals used in CUT assessments.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use the FeasibilityJudgment class for entities satisfying its intended role and modeled restrictions; supply relevant assertions explicitly.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Feasibility Judgment</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">General feasibility judgment class covering positive, negative, and insufficient-information outcomes; not restricted to a particular CUT test case</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">A judgment is the result assigned to one assessment. A distinct requirement-status relation is needed to separate evidence about requirements from the final Feasible, Infeasible, or InsufficientInformation outcome.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Feasible">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeasibilityJudgment"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">feasible</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Feasible.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feasible</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Feasible.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feasible</dcterms:source>
		<owl:disjointWith rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Infeasible"/>
		<owl:disjointWith rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/InsufficientInformation"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">feasibility judgment indicating that an assessed candidate–target–action configuration satisfies all applicable modeled action requirements under the represented conditions</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific definition representing the positive specialization of Feasibility Judgment when relevant action requirements are established as satisfied. Not directly defined by the supplied literature. Added during conceptual model revision. Lexical basis only: General feasibility concept only; the exact modeled requirements and judgment conditions are local. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A blade region has the required geometry, rigidity, and other relevant properties for the specified CUT action.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use only when the available property information is sufficient to establish that the relevant action requirements are met.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Feasible</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to a positive subclass of FeasibilityJudgment; it is not a physical property or an affordance of the object itself.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">For the current two-requirement CUT test, both rigidity and edge thickness requirements must be satisfied before a feasible judgment is derived. It is not a claim of universal feasibility.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureLevel">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">feature level</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">NIST Computer Security Resource Center, &quot;Ordinal Scale,&quot; Glossary. https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/ordinal_scale</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">NIST Computer Security Resource Center, &quot;Ordinal Scale,&quot; Glossary. https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/ordinal_scale</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">categorical level used to represent the degree of a physical feature, such as low, medium, or high</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from the concept of ordinal measurement scales. The ontology uses qualitative levels (Low, Medium, High) to categorize physical features. These levels are context-dependent and do not imply fixed numerical thresholds or equal intervals between categories. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">high is the qualitative level assigned to the rigidity value of testCandidateA.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use the FeatureLevel class for entities satisfying its intended role and modeled restrictions; supply relevant assertions explicitly.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Feature Level</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Feature levels (Low, Medium, and High) are qualitative categories used to describe physical features within a specified evaluation context. Their interpretation depends on the feature being evaluated and the modeled action conditions. The current course implementation uses controlled, explicitly assigned levels without universal numerical thresholds. Future extensions may introduce feature-specific quantitative criteria to support larger datasets and more diverse physical configurations.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Low, medium, and high are ordered qualitative descriptors, not fixed physical thresholds or equal numerical intervals. Their interpretation can depend on target configuration.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureValue">
		<rdfs:subClassOf>
			<owl:Restriction>
				<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasLevel"/>
				<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureLevel"/>
				<owl:qualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:qualifiedCardinality>
			</owl:Restriction>
		</rdfs:subClassOf>
		<rdfs:subClassOf>
			<owl:Restriction>
				<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasFeatureType"/>
				<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalFeature"/>
				<owl:qualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:qualifiedCardinality>
			</owl:Restriction>
		</rdfs:subClassOf>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">feature value</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">W3C, &quot;Semantic Sensor Network Ontology,&quot; W3C Recommendation, 2017. https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-ssn/</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">W3C, &quot;Semantic Sensor Network Ontology,&quot; W3C Recommendation, 2017. https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-ssn/</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">representation that associates a physical feature type with a categorical feature level for a physical entity</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Inspired by SOSA/SSN&apos;s separation of observable properties and their results. FeatureValue is a project-specific representation that associates a PhysicalFeature type with a qualitative FeatureLevel through hasFeatureType and hasLevel. It is not equivalent to a SOSA Observation or measurement result. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">testARigidityValue hasFeatureType rigidity and hasLevel high.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use the FeatureValue class for entities satisfying its intended role and modeled restrictions; supply relevant assertions explicitly.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Feature Value</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Used to represent the pairing of a physical feature type with a categorical feature level for a physical entity.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">A FeatureValue is an assertion-like individual linking a feature type and its qualitative level. It is distinct from a direct numeric observation or measured physical constant.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FlexibilityFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MechanicalFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">flexibility feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">P. P. Urone and R. Hinrichs, College Physics 2e, Section 5.3, &quot;Elasticity: Stress and Strain,&quot; OpenStax, 2022. https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/5-3-elasticity-stress-and-strain</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">P. P. Urone and R. Hinrichs, College Physics 2e, Section 5.3, &quot;Elasticity: Stress and Strain,&quot; OpenStax, 2022. https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/5-3-elasticity-stress-and-strain</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">mechanical feature describing the degree to which a physical entity can bend or deform without failure</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Implementation provenance: this term/label is represented in the project&apos;s internal research pipeline (scenarios.jsonl). These internal files are retained for project traceability and are not used as bibliographic sources. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from the treatment of elastic deformation and stiffness in OpenStax. The ontology represents flexibility as a qualitative physical feature describing the ability of an entity to bend or deform. This is a project-specific abstraction rather than a directly measured material constant.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT example: high Flexibility of a sponge&apos;s interacting part may allow substantial deformation under applied load, which can be relevant when evaluating a requirement that the working part maintain its geometry during a cutting attempt.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Used to represent known deformation behavior of an Object or Object Part when that behavior is relevant to an Action Requirement. Whether greater or lower Flexibility supports or conflicts with feasibility depends on the specified action, interacting part, target, loading configuration, and conditions.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Flexibility Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-independent mechanical-response property. Flexibility may depend on material behavior together with geometry, dimensions, loading direction, support conditions, and physical configuration. The current course implementation represents only flexibility information relevant to CUT, but Flexibility is not inherently an enabling or blocking property for that action.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Flexibility describes ease or extent of deformation under load and should be distinguished from softness, elasticity, and material identity. Softness concerns resistance to localized deformation or indentation, while elasticity concerns recovery of shape after a deforming load is removed. A flexible Object or Object Part may or may not be elastic, depending on its material behavior and deformation regime.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FlexibilityRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MechanicalRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">flexibility requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">mechanical requirement concerning the flexibility needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate flexibility value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning flexibility; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Flexibility Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to action requirements concerning flexibility.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">FlexibilityRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FrictionFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SurfaceFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">friction feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">OpenStax, College Physics 2e, Section 5.1, &apos;Friction,&apos; 2022. https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/5-1-friction Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, &apos;Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,&apos; Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2026, pp. 35930–35948. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">OpenStax, College Physics 2e, Section 5.1, &apos;Friction,&apos; 2022. https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/5-1-friction</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">surface feature describing resistance to sliding between contacting surfaces</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific extension of the SURFACE dimension. The paper does not define friction or a coefficient of friction. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from OpenStax&apos;s treatment of friction as a force opposing relative motion between contacting surfaces. FrictionFeature is a qualitative project-specific feature category, not an assertion of a measured frictional force or coefficient.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A supplied friction description concerns a candidate surface and a specified contacting surface.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Attach the known property to its actual physical bearer; use it only through evidence-backed requirement relationships.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Friction Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-independent property category. Known candidate-side values are supplied as input; object labels are not reasoning evidence.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Friction depends on the contacting surfaces and conditions. Smoothness alone does not determine friction.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FrictionRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SurfaceRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">friction requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">surface requirement concerning the friction needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate friction value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning friction; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Friction Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to action requirements concerning friction between contacting surfaces.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">FrictionRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/GeometricFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">geometric feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">ISO/TS 10303-1032:2024, &quot;Industrial automation systems and integration — Product data representation and exchange — Part 1032: Application module: Shape property assignment.&quot; https://www.iso.org/standard/89563.html</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">ISO/TS 10303-1032:2024, &quot;Industrial automation systems and integration — Product data representation and exchange — Part 1032: Application module: Shape property assignment.&quot; https://www.iso.org/standard/89563.html</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">physical feature describing the geometry, dimensions, or spatial form of a physical entity</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from ISO 10303&apos;s treatment of object shape and shape-related properties. GeometricFeature is a project-specific class for organizing geometry, dimensions, and spatial-form characteristics relevant to physical action feasibility. It does not implement the ISO application module directly. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative feature: the geometric of a relevant working part is represented by a FeatureValue.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use GeometricFeature to organize known feature types and their qualitative levels; attach feature values to the physical entity or working part that bears them.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Geometric Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Covers represented geometric, dimensional, or spatial-form characteristics of physical entities.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">GeometricFeature describes represented physical characteristics rather than a conventional object type. Its level can be supplied through FeatureValue assertions on the appropriate object or part.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/GeometricRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">geometric requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">action requirement concerning geometric characteristics needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate geometric value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning geometric; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Geometric Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Covers action requirements concerning geometric characteristics.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">GeometricRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/HardnessFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MechanicalFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">hardness feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST), &quot;Modeling, Simulation and Prediction of Rockwell Hardness Indentation.&quot; https://www.nist.gov/publications/modeling-simulation-and-prediction-rockwell-hardness-indentation</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST), &quot;Modeling, Simulation and Prediction of Rockwell Hardness Indentation.&quot; https://www.nist.gov/publications/modeling-simulation-and-prediction-rockwell-hardness-indentation</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">mechanical feature describing resistance to localized deformation or indentation</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from the materials-science concept of hardness as resistance to localized plastic deformation. The ontology represents hardness at a qualitative feature level rather than as a standardized indentation measurement. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative feature: the hardness of a relevant working part is represented by a FeatureValue.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use HardnessFeature to organize known feature types and their qualitative levels; attach feature values to the physical entity or working part that bears them.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Hardness Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to hardness represented as a mechanical feature.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">HardnessFeature describes represented physical characteristics rather than a conventional object type. Its level can be supplied through FeatureValue assertions on the appropriate object or part.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/HighHardnessRigidityMapping">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/RequirementMapping"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">high hardness rigidity mapping</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<owl:equivalentClass>
			<owl:Class>
				<owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
					<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/RequirementMapping">
					</rdf:Description>
					<owl:Restriction>
						<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/affectsRequirement"/>
						<owl:hasValue rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/cutRigidityRequirement"/>
					</owl:Restriction>
					<owl:Restriction>
						<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/specifiesRequiredLevel"/>
						<owl:hasValue rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/high"/>
					</owl:Restriction>
					<owl:Restriction>
						<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/usesTargetFeatureType"/>
						<owl:hasValue rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hardness"/>
					</owl:Restriction>
					<owl:Restriction>
						<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/usesTargetLevel"/>
						<owl:hasValue rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/high"/>
					</owl:Restriction>
				</owl:intersectionOf>
			</owl:Class>
		</owl:equivalentClass>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">requirement mapping that connects high target hardness to a high required rigidity level for cutting</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Team-defined CUT-specific OWL mapping. The high-hardness → high-rigidity rule is an explicit assumption of the controlled test model, not a universal materials-science law. Lexical basis only: Only the general requirement meaning is borrowed; high-hardness-to-high-rigidity mapping is a local CUT test-specific class. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">The high target hardness condition in cutRigidityMapping triggers a high rigidity requirement in the current test.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class when the stated structural or assessment restrictions are supported by explicitly modeled assertions.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">High Hardness Rigidity Mapping</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Applies to the represented high-hardness target condition and CUT rigidity requirement; does not by itself establish overall CUT feasibility.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">The mapping encodes a simplified requirement conditioned on the target hardness. It is a controlled CUT assumption and should not be interpreted as universal cutting physics.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Infeasible">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeasibilityJudgment"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">infeasible</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Infeasible.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/infeasible</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Infeasible.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/infeasible</dcterms:source>
		<owl:disjointWith rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/InsufficientInformation"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">feasibility judgment indicating that at least one applicable action requirement conflicts with the represented physical features of the assessed candidate–target–action configuration</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific definition representing the negative specialization of Feasibility Judgment when one or more relevant action requirements are established as unsatisfied or conflicting. Not directly defined by the supplied literature. Added during conceptual model revision. Lexical basis only: General infeasibility concept only; the conflict-based inference criterion is project-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">An object region is too flexible to satisfy a rigidity requirement necessary for the specified CUT action.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use when evidence establishes failure or conflict with one or more requirements, not merely when required information is absent.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Infeasible</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to a negative subclass of FeasibilityJudgment where available evidence supports a negative feasibility determination.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">An explicitly conflicting modeled requirement is sufficient for the intended negative judgment. Infeasible must not be confused with information that is merely unspecified.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/InsufficientInformation">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeasibilityJudgment"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">insufficient information</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">feasibility judgment indicating that no applicable modeled requirement is known to conflict, but at least one requirement is explicitly unresolved, preventing a determination of feasibility</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific definition representing an indeterminate Feasibility Judgment when available evidence is insufficient to establish either feasibility or infeasibility. This category is introduced by the project rather than directly defined in the supplied literature. Added during conceptual model revision. Lexical basis only: An evaluation may remain inconclusive, but this source does not define the local InsufficientInformation outcome or SWRL conditions. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">In CUT Test C, the candidate&apos;s rigidity is high, but its edge thickness is explicitly unspecified; the assessment therefore receives an insufficient-information judgment under the implemented rules.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use when available information cannot establish either Feasible or Infeasible.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Insufficient Information</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to an indeterminate subclass of FeasibilityJudgment caused by inadequate information; it does not mean that the action is physically infeasible.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">InsufficientInformation is intended when there is no explicit conflicting requirement and one or more applicable requirements are unresolved. RDF absence alone does not establish unresolved status under OWL open-world semantics.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/InteractingPart">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">interacting part</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Interact.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/interact</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:contributor xml:lang="en">LZ</dcterms:contributor>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Interact.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/interact</dcterms:source>
		<owl:equivalentClass>
			<owl:Class>
				<owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
					<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ObjectPart">
					</rdf:Description>
					<owl:Restriction>
						<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/interactsWith"/>
						<owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ObjectPart"/>
					</owl:Restriction>
				</owl:intersectionOf>
			</owl:Class>
		</owl:equivalentClass>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">object part that physically interacts with another object part relevant to the represented action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific OWL defined class in OE9. Defined as an Object Part that has an interactsWith relationship to another Object Part. Lexical basis only: Supports interaction in general; InteractingPart is locally defined by OWL object-property restrictions. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative InteractingPart case: a test object or assessment meeting the relevant class restrictions.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class when the stated structural or assessment restrictions are supported by explicitly modeled assertions.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Interacting Part</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Applies to object parts involved in represented physical interactions; classification alone does not establish overall CUT feasibility.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">InteractingPart is a locally defined structural or reasoning classification; check its OWL axioms before generalizing from observed test cases.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/InteractingPartObject">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">object with interacting parts</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Interact.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/interact</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:contributor xml:lang="en">LZ</dcterms:contributor>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Interact.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/interact</dcterms:source>
		<owl:equivalentClass>
			<owl:Class>
				<owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
					<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/WholeObject">
					</rdf:Description>
					<owl:Restriction>
						<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasPart"/>
						<owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/InteractingPart"/>
					</owl:Restriction>
				</owl:intersectionOf>
			</owl:Class>
		</owl:equivalentClass>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">object having at least one part that interacts with another object part</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific OWL defined class. Equivalent to a WholeObject with hasPart some InteractingPart. Lexical basis only: Supports interaction in general; object-with-interacting-parts OWL classification is local. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative InteractingPartObject case: a test object or assessment meeting the relevant class restrictions.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class when the stated structural or assessment restrictions are supported by explicitly modeled assertions.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Object with Interacting Parts</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Classifies whole objects having at least one represented interacting part; membership alone does not establish CUT feasibility.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">InteractingPartObject is a locally defined structural or reasoning classification; check its OWL axioms before generalizing from observed test cases.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/JointFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/StructuralFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">joint feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Z. Wang, Y. Zhong, J. Fu, H. I. Christensen, and J. Gao, &quot;AFUN: Towards an Affordance Foundation Model for Functionality Understanding,&quot; arXiv:2606.02551, 2026. https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.02551 Cambridge Dictionary, “Structure.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/structure</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Structure.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/structure</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">structural feature describing a connection between parts of a physical entity</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific subclass of StructuralFeature in the OE9 RDF. AFUN provides background on articulated structures and motion constraints but does not define this ontology class or its exact label. Lexical basis only: General parts-and-structure sense only; JointFeature and any inferred structural conditions are local. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A hinge connecting a lid and a container constrains the lid&apos;s movement relative to the container.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use JointFeature to describe structural characteristics of connections between parts. Use RotationalStructureFeature when the characteristic specifically concerns relative rotational movement.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Joint Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to structural characteristics of a connection between object parts; it does not denote the connected parts themselves or the action performed using them.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">In OE9, JointFeature is a subclass of StructuralFeature and describes a connection between parts. The interactsWith relationship between object parts is represented separately and does not itself define membership in JointFeature.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/JointRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/StructuralRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">joint requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">structural requirement concerning a connection between parts needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate joint value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning joint; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Joint Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to structural requirements concerning connections between parts.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">JointRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MaterialFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">material feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>B. Barati and E. Karana, “Affordances as Materials Potential: What Design Can Do for Materials Development,” International Journal of Design, vol. 13, no. 3, pp. 105–123, 2019. D. Roylance, “Introduction to Elastic Response,” Mechanics of Materials, Massachusetts Institute of Technology OpenCourseWare, Cambridge, MA, USA, 2000 Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” Findings of ACL: EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>a characteristic of the substance or composition from which a physical entity or its component is made</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Round 2 revision distinguishes Material Property from object geometry, dimensions, conventional identity, and affordance roles. Whether a Material Property enables or blocks an action depends on its relationship to the applicable Action Requirement and physical context. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Physical properties of materials are used as evidence in the cited benchmark. MaterialFeature is a Team Affordance category for material characteristics; the source motivates this distinction but does not formally define the OWL class. Dictionary entry supplies only the ordinary meaning of the physical characteristic. AFFORDANCE20Q is retained as background motivation, not as the source of this exact OWL class definition. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT example: Shear Strength of a target material is a Material Property that may be relevant when evaluating whether the applied loading can produce material separation under the specified cutting conditions</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Used to represent known characteristics of the material associated with an Object or Object Part when those characteristics may be evaluated against an Action Requirement. Material Property itself does not indicate whether the characteristic enables or blocks an action; that role depends on the applicable requirement and physical context.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Material Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-general physical-property concept. Material Properties may include mechanical, thermal, electrical, or other material-dependent characteristics, but the current course implementation represents only properties relevant to physical feasibility of CUT. A property&apos;s relevance to an action is represented separately from the property itself.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">A Material Property characterizes the material from which an Object or Object Part is composed and should be distinguished from object-level geometry, shape, dimensions, identity, and interaction configuration. Material properties may depend on material state, microstructure, orientation, temperature, moisture, or other specified conditions; therefore, “material property” does not imply a universally constant value.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MaterialRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">material requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” Findings of ACL: EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">action requirement concerning a material characteristic needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Adapted from MADE_OF predicates and material-composition annotations. The requirement class is project-specific. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Parent aligned with Diagram 1. Requirement class created by Team Affordance to specify a condition over a modeled feature; AFFORDANCE20Q motivates physical-property evidence but does not define this OWL class. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A pathway records a material-composition constraint only when supported by the project’s evidence.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Compare supplied facts with the represented requirement. Explicit conflict and missing information are distinct outcomes.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Material Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-general requirement category. Its specific constraints depend on the action and represented physical conditions. Target-conditioned required levels may be expressed through RequirementMapping when explicitly modeled; no fixed threshold is implied by this class alone.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">A material name is evidence of composition. It does not automatically establish rigidity, strength, or CUT support.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MechanicalFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">mechanical feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “AFFORDANCE20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” arXiv:2606.14240v2, 2026., OpenStax, University Physics Volume 1, Section 12.3, &quot;Stress, Strain, and Elastic Modulus.&quot; https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-3-stress-strain-and-elastic-modulus</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">OpenStax, University Physics Volume 1, Section 12.3, &quot;Stress, Strain, and Elastic Modulus.&quot; https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/12-3-stress-strain-and-elastic-modulus</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">physical feature describing the mechanical behavior of a physical entity under applied forces</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Adapted from rigidity and flexibility, grouped under MATERIAL in the paper. The separate mechanical category follows the conceptual model. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from the mechanics of material deformation under applied forces. The ontology groups physical characteristics such as rigidity, flexibility, and strength under MechanicalFeature. This grouping is project-specific and does not imply that all mechanical features are equivalent or interchangeable.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Known rigidity of a candidate blade part.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Attach the known property to its actual physical bearer; use it only through evidence-backed requirement relationships.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Mechanical Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-independent property category. Known candidate-side values are supplied as input; object labels are not reasoning evidence.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Rigidity, flexibility, and strength are distinct mechanical characteristics. None alone establishes CUT support.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MechanicalRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">mechanical requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” Findings of ACL: EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">action requirement concerning mechanical behavior needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Adapted from the paper’s rigidity/flexibility evidence. A separate mechanical requirement category is a project extension. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Parent aligned with Diagram 1. Requirement class created by Team Affordance to specify a condition over a modeled feature; AFFORDANCE20Q motivates physical-property evidence but does not define this OWL class. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A modeled CUT pathway requires a working part to retain its relevant geometry under the represented load.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Compare supplied facts with the represented requirement. Explicit conflict and missing information are distinct outcomes.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Mechanical Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-general requirement category. Its specific constraints depend on the action and represented physical conditions. Target-conditioned required levels may be expressed through RequirementMapping when explicitly modeled; no fixed threshold is implied by this class alone.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Mechanical constraints may concern rigidity, flexibility, or strength. Their relevance depends on the represented pathway.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MultiPartObject">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">multi-part object</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">OBO Relation Ontology (RO), &quot;has part&quot; (BFO:0000051). https://oborel.github.io/obo-relations/ro-and-bfo/</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:contributor xml:lang="en">LZ</dcterms:contributor>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">OBO Relation Ontology (RO), &quot;has part&quot; (BFO:0000051). https://oborel.github.io/obo-relations/ro-and-bfo/</dcterms:source>
		<owl:equivalentClass>
			<owl:Class>
				<owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
					<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/WholeObject">
					</rdf:Description>
					<owl:Restriction>
						<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasPart"/>
						<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ObjectPart"/>
						<owl:minQualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">2</owl:minQualifiedCardinality>
					</owl:Restriction>
				</owl:intersectionOf>
			</owl:Class>
		</owl:equivalentClass>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">object with at least two distinct represented object parts</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific OWL defined class. Equivalent to a WholeObject with hasPart min 2 ObjectPart. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Conceptually informed by the generic part–whole relation in RO. The threshold of at least two distinct ObjectPart fillers is a project-specific OWL cardinality condition; MultiPartObject is not equivalent to an RO or BFO class. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A structural test object with two explicitly distinct part individuals is used to exercise multipart classification.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class when the stated structural or assessment restrictions are supported by explicitly modeled assertions.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Multi-Part Object</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Requires at least two distinct ObjectPart fillers in the represented part relation; this is an OWL qualified minimum-cardinality condition.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">A cardinality restriction stating at least two distinct parts is not proved by two names alone if the individuals may denote the same part under OWL semantics.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MultiPartStructureFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/StructuralFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">multi part structure feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “AFFORDANCE20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” arXiv:2606.14240v2, 2026. Cambridge Dictionary, “Structure.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/structure</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Structure.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/structure</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">structural feature describing an arrangement in which multiple parts jointly participate in a physical configuration</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific subclass of StructuralFeature defined in the OE9 RDF. Myers et al. and AFFORDANCE20Q provide background on tool parts and structure-based affordance reasoning, rather than the exact OWL definition. Lexical basis only: Supports general arrangement-of-parts terminology; MultiPartStructureFeature is a local OWL subclass. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A tool consisting of a handle and a working part has a multipart structure whose arrangement contributes to how the tool can be used.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use MultiPartStructureFeature when the relevant physical characteristic depends on the presence or arrangement of multiple parts. Use JointFeature when the connection between particular parts is the focus.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Multi Part Structure Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to structural characteristics arising from the composition or arrangement of multiple object parts. It does not require the parts to be movable or articulated.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Myers et al. describe tools as composed of multiple parts, while AFFORDANCE20Q discusses physical properties relevant to affordance reasoning.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MultiPartStructureRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/StructuralRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">multi part structure requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">structural requirement concerning the use or arrangement of multiple parts needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate multi part structure value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning multi part structure; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Multi Part Structure Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to structural requirements involving the use or arrangement of multiple parts.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">MultiPartStructureRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ObjectPart">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalEntity"/>
		<rdfs:subClassOf>
			<owl:Restriction>
				<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/isPartOf"/>
				<owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalEntity"/>
			</owl:Restriction>
		</rdfs:subClassOf>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">object part</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “AFFORDANCE20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” arXiv:2606.14240v2, 2026. A. Myers, C. L. Teo, C. Fermüller, and Y. Aloimonos, “Affordance Detection of Tool Parts from Geometric Features,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Robotics and Automation (ICRA), Seattle, WA, USA, 2015, pp. 1374–1381, doi: 10.1109/ICRA.2015.7139369. OBO Foundry, “Relations Ontology (RO),” [Online]. Available: https://obofoundry.org/ontology/ro.html.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:contributor xml:lang="en">LZ</dcterms:contributor>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">OBO Relations Ontology, “part of” relation, BFO:0000050; A. Myers, C. L. Teo, C. Fermüller, and Y. Aloimonos, “Affordance Detection of Tool Parts from Geometric Features,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Robotics and Automation (ICRA), Seattle, WA, USA, 2015, pp. 1374–1381, doi: 10.1109/ICRA.2015.7139369.</dcterms:source>
		<owl:disjointWith rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/WholeObject"/>
		<skos:definition>a constituent component of a material object that is represented separately and may have its own physical feature values and structural relationships</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Implementation provenance: this term/label is represented in the project&apos;s internal research pipeline (prompts.py; step3_generate_contrasts.py). These internal files are retained for project traceability and are not used as bibliographic sources. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Project-specific working definition synthesized from the general part–whole relation in the Relations Ontology and affordance literature that treats local object parts as separately relevant for interaction and function; not a verbatim definition from either source.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT example: the blades and handles of a pair of scissors are distinct Object Parts; the blade properties are relevant to the cutting interaction, while the handle properties support manipulation by the actor</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Used to attach known Physical Properties to the local component or region that actually participates in an action and to distinguish those properties from properties of the whole Object. Object Parts may be compared across alternative configurations when the relevant interacting part changes</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Object Part</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-general structural concept. An Object Part may be a physically distinct component or a locally identified region of an object, depending on the representation used. The current ontology represents parts only when their known physical properties or interaction role are relevant to action-feasibility reasoning</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Different Object Parts of the same Object may have different physical properties and may therefore participate differently in action feasibility. Object Part should not be interpreted as a subclass of Object merely because a part belongs to an object; the intended semantics are part–whole. The ontology may use an existing part-of relation rather than inventing a project-specific relation where suitable</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PairedInteractingObject">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">object with paired interacting parts</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Interact.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/interact</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:contributor xml:lang="en">LZ</dcterms:contributor>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Interact.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/interact</dcterms:source>
		<owl:equivalentClass>
			<owl:Class>
				<owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
					<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/WholeObject">
					</rdf:Description>
					<owl:Restriction>
						<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasPart"/>
						<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/InteractingPart"/>
						<owl:minQualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">2</owl:minQualifiedCardinality>
					</owl:Restriction>
				</owl:intersectionOf>
			</owl:Class>
		</owl:equivalentClass>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">object with at least two distinct parts that each interact with another part</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific OWL defined class. Equivalent to a WholeObject with hasPart min 2 InteractingPart. Lexical basis only: Supports interaction in general; paired part and cardinality conditions are local. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative PairedInteractingObject case: a test object or assessment meeting the relevant class restrictions.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class when the stated structural or assessment restrictions are supported by explicitly modeled assertions.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Object with Paired Interacting Parts</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Requires at least two distinct InteractingPart fillers; the OWL definition does not require those two parts to interact specifically with each other.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">PairedInteractingObject is a locally defined structural or reasoning classification; check its OWL axioms before generalizing from observed test cases.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PartBearingObject">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">part-bearing object</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">OBO Relation Ontology (RO), &quot;has part&quot; (BFO:0000051). https://oborel.github.io/obo-relations/ro-and-bfo/</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:contributor xml:lang="en">LZ</dcterms:contributor>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">OBO Relation Ontology (RO), &quot;has part&quot; (BFO:0000051). https://oborel.github.io/obo-relations/ro-and-bfo/</dcterms:source>
		<owl:equivalentClass>
			<owl:Class>
				<owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
					<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/WholeObject">
					</rdf:Description>
					<owl:Restriction>
						<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasPart"/>
						<owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ObjectPart"/>
					</owl:Restriction>
				</owl:intersectionOf>
			</owl:Class>
		</owl:equivalentClass>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">object with at least one represented object part</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific OWL defined class. Equivalent to a WholeObject with hasPart some ObjectPart. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Conceptually informed by RO’s has-part relation. PartBearingObject is a local classification of a WholeObject with at least one ObjectPart; the specific OWL class and its restriction are defined by Team Affordance. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative PartBearingObject case: a test object or assessment meeting the relevant class restrictions.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class when the stated structural or assessment restrictions are supported by explicitly modeled assertions.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Part-Bearing Object</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Applies when at least one ObjectPart is represented through hasPart; does not determine action feasibility by itself.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">PartBearingObject is a locally defined structural or reasoning classification; check its OWL axioms before generalizing from observed test cases.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalEntity">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">physical entity</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Basic Formal Ontology (BFO), &quot;material entity&quot; (BFO:0000040). http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000040</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">Basic Formal Ontology (BFO), &quot;material entity&quot; (BFO:0000040). http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000040</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>a tangible object or constituent component that may participate in a modeled physical operation as a candidate, target, or component of another object</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific superclass in the OE9 ontology. WholeObject and ObjectPart are modeled as subclasses of PhysicalEntity. The earlier conceptual-model classes PhysicalObject and ObjectRegion are not part of the final OE9 class hierarchy. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from BFO&apos;s concept of a material entity. PhysicalEntity is used as a general class for physical objects and object parts participating in action assessments. Candidate and target are context-dependent roles represented through assessment relationships, rather than intrinsic object categories. The class is not declared equivalent to BFO Material Entity. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">knifeCandidate is represented as a WholeObject with knifeBlade as an ObjectPart connected through hasPart. Both belong to the PhysicalEntity hierarchy.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use WholeObject for a complete candidate or target and ObjectPart for its constituent parts. Represent physical evidence through feature values and part relationships rather than object identity or conventional function.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Physical Entity</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Covers physical entities represented in the CUT model, including complete candidate or target objects and their constituent parts. Being a PhysicalEntity does not itself establish action feasibility.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">In OE9, PhysicalEntity is the superclass of WholeObject and ObjectPart. The hasFeatureValue property links a physical entity to FeatureValue, which identifies a PhysicalFeature through hasFeatureType. The hasPart property links a physical entity to its object parts.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalFeature">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">physical feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, &quot;Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,&quot; Findings of EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">represented physical characteristic of a physical entity used in evaluating action feasibility</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Synthesized from the source&apos;s use of physical properties as evidence for affordance reasoning. PhysicalFeature is a project-specific ontology class used to organize represented geometric, mechanical, material, surface, and structural characteristics. The source motivates this representation but does not formally define the OWL class. Dictionary entry supplies only the ordinary meaning of the physical characteristic. AFFORDANCE20Q is retained as background motivation, not as the source of this exact OWL class definition. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">rigidity and edgeThickness are physical feature type individuals referenced by CUT requirements.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use PhysicalFeature to organize known feature types and their qualitative levels; attach feature values to the physical entity or working part that bears them.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Physical Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Covers represented physical characteristics of physical entities used as evidence in action-feasibility reasoning.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Feature types classify explicitly represented physical characteristics. A candidate name, object category, or conventional function is not itself a physical feature used as feasibility evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PointGeometryFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/GeometricFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">point geometry feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” Findings of ACL: EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Shape.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/shape</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Shape.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/shape</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">geometric feature describing the shape or form of a point or tip of a physical entity</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Adapted from pointed and tapered part predicates in pierce_through rule examples. These examples do not establish CUT requirements. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): This source supports treating physical characteristics as evidence for affordance reasoning. Team Affordance defines PointGeometryFeature locally as: &quot;geometric feature describing the shape or form of a point or tip of a physical entity&quot;. The publication is conceptual motivation, not a formal OWL class definition. Dictionary entry supplies only the ordinary meaning of the physical characteristic. AFFORDANCE20Q is retained as background motivation, not as the source of this exact OWL class definition.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A candidate tip is described as pointed, tapered, and thin.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Attach the known property to its actual physical bearer; use it only through evidence-backed requirement relationships.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Point Geometry Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-independent property category. Known candidate-side values are supplied as input; object labels are not reasoning evidence.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Point geometry is distinct from edge geometry. Its CUT relevance requires separate project evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PointGeometryRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/GeometricRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">point geometry requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">geometric requirement concerning the point or tip geometry needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate point geometry value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning point geometry; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Point Geometry Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to action requirements concerning point or tip geometry.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">PointGeometryRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/RequirementMapping">
		<rdfs:subClassOf>
			<owl:Restriction>
				<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/affectsRequirement"/>
				<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement"/>
				<owl:qualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:qualifiedCardinality>
			</owl:Restriction>
		</rdfs:subClassOf>
		<rdfs:subClassOf>
			<owl:Restriction>
				<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/specifiesRequiredLevel"/>
				<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureLevel"/>
				<owl:qualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:qualifiedCardinality>
			</owl:Restriction>
		</rdfs:subClassOf>
		<rdfs:subClassOf>
			<owl:Restriction>
				<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/usesTargetLevel"/>
				<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureLevel"/>
				<owl:qualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:qualifiedCardinality>
			</owl:Restriction>
		</rdfs:subClassOf>
		<rdfs:subClassOf>
			<owl:Restriction>
				<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/usesTargetFeatureType"/>
				<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalFeature"/>
				<owl:qualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:qualifiedCardinality>
			</owl:Restriction>
		</rdfs:subClassOf>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">requirement mapping</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">mapping that relates a target feature condition to a required feature level for an action requirement</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: The dictionary supports requirement terminology, not the RequirementMapping OWL class or its target-dependent mapping. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">cutRigidityMapping relates target hardness to the rigidity requirement.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use the RequirementMapping class for entities satisfying its intended role and modeled restrictions; supply relevant assertions explicitly.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Requirement Mapping</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to mappings that derive a required feature level from a represented target-feature condition.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">A mapping uses the represented target feature and level to determine the applicable feature requirement for an action. The specific high-hardness/high-rigidity rule is a controlled test assumption, not a universal material law.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/RigidityFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MechanicalFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">rigidity feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">P. P. Urone and R. Hinrichs, College Physics 2e, Section 5.3, &quot;Elasticity: Stress and Strain,&quot; OpenStax, 2022. https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/5-3-elasticity-stress-and-strain</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">P. P. Urone and R. Hinrichs, College Physics 2e, Section 5.3, &quot;Elasticity: Stress and Strain,&quot; OpenStax, 2022. https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/5-3-elasticity-stress-and-strain</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">mechanical feature describing the degree to which a physical entity resists bending or deformation</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Implementation provenance: this term/label is represented in the project&apos;s internal research pipeline (scenarios.jsonl; contrasts.jsonl; property_canonical_map.csv). These internal files are retained for project traceability and are not used as bibliographic sources. Adapted from AFFORDANCE20Q’s classification of rigidity/flexibility within the MATERIAL physical-property dimension and its use of “rigid” or “rigid_material” as a static property in affordance rules; the source uses the property operationally rather than supplying a standalone mechanical definition of rigidity. An additional preferred source listed in the curated terminology (Hyejin’s project dataset) was not available for review. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from the mechanics of deformation and stiffness under applied forces. The ontology represents rigidity as a qualitative feature of a physical entity or relevant part, rather than equating it directly with Young&apos;s modulus. The represented level may depend on both material and geometry.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A pierce-through rule includes a part made of rigid material together with pointed shape and thin size.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use as a material-related property in physical prerequisites.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Rigidity Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">RigidityFeature is modeled as a subclass of MechanicalFeature in the current ontology. It describes resistance to deformation and may depend on material characteristics, geometry, dimensions, and loading conditions. Rigidity is not inherently an enabling feature; its relevance to feasibility depends on the action requirements and the target conditions.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Rigidity can combine with geometric properties to support an affordance; it is not treated as an affordance by itself.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/RigidityRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MechanicalRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">rigidity requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">mechanical requirement concerning the rigidity needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate rigidity value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning rigidity; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Rigidity Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to action requirements concerning rigidity.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">RigidityRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/RotationalStructureFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/StructuralFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">rotational structure feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Z. Wang, Y. Zhong, J. Fu, H. I. Christensen, and J. Gao, &quot;AFUN: Towards an Affordance Foundation Model for Functionality Understanding,&quot; arXiv:2606.02551, 2026. https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.02551 Cambridge Dictionary, “Structure.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/structure</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Structure.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/structure</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">structural feature describing an arrangement that permits or constrains relative rotational movement between parts</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific subclass of StructuralFeature defined in the OE9 RDF. AFUN provides background on articulated objects, motion axes, and rotational parameters; the exact ontology class and definition are project-specific. Lexical basis only: Supports general organization of components; the rotational feature category is local. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A hinged lid has a structural arrangement that permits rotation about the hinge axis.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use for structural rotational capability or constraint, not for an observed rotational action or trajectory.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Rotational Structure Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to structural characteristics that permit, constrain, or define rotational movement of one part relative to another.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">AFUN discusses articulated-object manipulation using motion types, axes, and motion parameters; this provides supporting domain evidence for treating rotational capability as a structural characteristic.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/RotationalStructureRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/StructuralRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">rotational structure requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">structural requirement concerning rotational relationships between parts needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate rotational structure value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning rotational structure; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Rotational Structure Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to structural requirements concerning rotational relationships between parts.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">RotationalStructureRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ShapeFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/GeometricFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">shape feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">B. Barati and E. Karana, “Affordances as Materials Potential: What Design Can Do for Materials Development,” International Journal of Design, vol. 13, no. 3, pp. 105–123, 2019. Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “AFFORDANCE20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” arXiv:2606.14240, 2026. Z. Wang, Y. Zhong, J. Fu, H. I. Christensen, and J. Gao, “AFUN: Towards an Affordance Foundation Model for Functionality Understanding,” arXiv:2606.02551, 2026. A. Myers, C. L. Teo, C. Fermüller, and Y. Aloimonos, “Affordance Detection of Tool Parts from Geometric Features,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Robotics and Automation (ICRA), Seattle, WA, USA, 2015, pp. 1374–1381, doi: 10.1109/ICRA.2015.7139369. OBO Foundry, “Phenotype and Trait Ontology (PATO): Shape (PATO:0000052),” [Online]. Available: https://obofoundry.org/ontology/pato.html.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">Phenotype and Trait Ontology (PATO), “shape” (PATO:0000052), OBO Foundry; A. Myers, C. L. Teo, C. Fermüller, and Y. Aloimonos, “Affordance Detection of Tool Parts from Geometric Features,” in Proc. IEEE Int. Conf. Robotics and Automation (ICRA), Seattle, WA, USA, 2015, pp. 1374–1381, doi: 10.1109/ICRA.2015.7139369</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>a geometric characteristic describing the external form or spatial contour of a physical entity</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Round 2 revision aligns Shape with the Physical Property hierarchy and distinguishes geometric form from material-dependent properties, dimensional properties, and contextual affordance relations. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Project-specific working definition adapted from PATO&apos;s treatment of shape as a morphological quality determined by spatial relationships among an entity&apos;s features, with affordance literature used to establish the relevance of local shape and geometry to interaction; not a verbatim definition from either source Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT example: the geometry of a card edge and the broader form of the card may provide Shape information relevant to a cutting configuration, but feasibility depends on those geometric characteristics together with other properties and conditions.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Used to represent known geometric characteristics of an Object or Object Part when those characteristics may be evaluated against an Action Requirement. Shape alone does not establish action feasibility and is not inherently an Enabling Property or Blocking Property</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Shape Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-independent geometric property. Shape may characterize an Object or Object Part and may be relevant to different Action Requirements depending on the specified action and physical context. The current course implementation represents only CUT-relevant shape information, but Shape is not restricted to CUT or to a particular affordance.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Shape describes geometric form and should be distinguished from material-dependent properties, overall size or thickness, and contextual affordance relations. Source datasets may group a broad range of geometric and topological descriptors under a SHAPE dimension, but this operational grouping does not require the ontology to treat all such descriptors as subclasses of Shape</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ShapeRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/GeometricRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">shape requirement</rdfs:label>
