		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate shape value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning shape; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Shape Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to action requirements concerning shape.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">ShapeRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SinglePartObject">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">single-part object</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">OBO Relation Ontology (RO), &quot;has part&quot; (BFO:0000051). https://oborel.github.io/obo-relations/ro-and-bfo/</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:contributor xml:lang="en">LZ</dcterms:contributor>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">OBO Relation Ontology (RO), &quot;has part&quot; (BFO:0000051). https://oborel.github.io/obo-relations/ro-and-bfo/</dcterms:source>
		<owl:equivalentClass>
			<owl:Class>
				<owl:intersectionOf rdf:parseType="Collection">
					<rdf:Description rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/WholeObject">
					</rdf:Description>
					<owl:Restriction>
						<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasPart"/>
						<owl:onClass rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ObjectPart"/>
						<owl:qualifiedCardinality rdf:datatype="&xs;nonNegativeInteger">1</owl:qualifiedCardinality>
					</owl:Restriction>
				</owl:intersectionOf>
			</owl:Class>
		</owl:equivalentClass>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">object constrained to have exactly one object part</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific OWL defined class. Equivalent to a WholeObject with hasPart exactly 1 ObjectPart. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Conceptually informed by RO’s part–whole relation. The exactly-one ObjectPart restriction is a project-specific OWL modeling decision; under open-world semantics, one observed part alone does not prove that no others exist. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">An object constrained to exactly one ObjectPart is an example of SinglePartObject.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class when the stated structural or assessment restrictions are supported by explicitly modeled assertions.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Single-Part Object</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Uses an OWL qualified exact-cardinality restriction, not merely the observation that one part has been listed.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">OWL cardinality restrictions use open-world semantics. One asserted part does not prove that an entity has exactly one part unless additional constraints or explicit distinctness facts support the inference.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SizeFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/GeometricFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">size feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">B. Barati and E. Karana, &apos;Affordances as materials potential: What design can do for materials development,&apos; International Journal of Design, 13(3), 105–123, 2019. https://ijdesign.org/index.php/IJDesign/article/view/3419/879 Phenotype and Trait Ontology (PATO), &apos;size&apos; (PATO:0000117). http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000117</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">Phenotype and Trait Ontology (PATO), &quot;size&quot; (PATO:0000117). http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000117</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">geometric feature describing a dimensional extent of a physical entity</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Adapted from AFFORDANCE20Q’s SIZE dimension, which covers dimensional characteristics such as how big, long, thick, or small an object is and preserves comparative size descriptions; not a verbatim definition. An additional preferred source listed in the curated terminology (Liangliang’s MAT source) was not available for review. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from PATO&apos;s representation of size as a quality associated with physical magnitude. SizeFeature is a project-specific class for representing dimensional characteristics of physical entities and their parts. The ontology uses qualitative feature levels rather than requiring numerical measurements. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Thin, long, pocket-sized, larger_than_hand, and smaller_than_coin are SIZE descriptions used in the benchmark.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use as the parent category for dimensional evidence.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Size Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">SIZE is one of four allowed physical-property dimensions and may include comparative descriptions.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">The Checker preserves comparison direction and reference objects instead of collapsing comparative size questions to a generic label.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SizeRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/GeometricRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">size requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
