		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate size value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning size; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Size Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to action requirements concerning dimensional extent.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">SizeRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SmoothnessFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SurfaceFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">smoothness feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” Findings of ACL: EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Surface.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/surface</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Surface.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/surface</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">surface feature describing the degree to which a surface lacks roughness or irregularity</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Adapted from SURFACE:smooth and smoothness questions. The degree-based definition is project-specific; the paper gives no measurement threshold. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): This source supports treating physical characteristics as evidence for affordance reasoning. Team Affordance defines SmoothnessFeature locally as: &quot;surface feature describing the degree to which a surface lacks roughness or irregularity&quot;. The publication is conceptual motivation, not a formal OWL class definition. Dictionary entry supplies only the ordinary meaning of the physical characteristic. AFFORDANCE20Q is retained as background motivation, not as the source of this exact OWL class definition.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A supplied candidate surface is described as smooth.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Attach the known property to its actual physical bearer; use it only through evidence-backed requirement relationships.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Smoothness Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-independent property category. Known candidate-side values are supplied as input; object labels are not reasoning evidence.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Smoothness describes texture. It is distinct from friction and does not by itself establish CUT support.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SmoothnessRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SurfaceRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">smoothness requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">surface requirement concerning the smoothness needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate smoothness value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning smoothness; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Smoothness Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to action requirements concerning surface smoothness.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">SmoothnessRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/StrengthFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MechanicalFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">strength feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” Findings of ACL: EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">mechanical feature describing resistance to failure under an applied force</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific working definition. AFFORDANCE20Q supplies the physical-property framework but does not define strength or its failure criteria. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): This source supports treating physical characteristics as evidence for affordance reasoning. Team Affordance defines StrengthFeature locally as: &quot;mechanical feature describing resistance to failure under an applied force&quot;. The publication is conceptual motivation, not a formal OWL class definition. Dictionary entry supplies only the ordinary meaning of the physical characteristic. AFFORDANCE20Q is retained as background motivation, not as the source of this exact OWL class definition.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A supplied strength property records a candidate part’s resistance to a specified failure mode.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Attach the known property to its actual physical bearer; use it only through evidence-backed requirement relationships.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Strength Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-independent property category. Known candidate-side values are supplied as input; object labels are not reasoning evidence.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Strength concerns yielding or failure. It differs from rigidity and must specify a loading mode or failure criterion.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/StrengthRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/MechanicalRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">strength requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">mechanical requirement concerning the strength needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate strength value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning strength; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Strength Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to action requirements concerning mechanical strength.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">StrengthRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/StructuralFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">structural feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” Findings of ACL: EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Structure.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/structure</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Structure.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/structure</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">physical feature describing the organization, arrangement, or relationship of parts within a physical entity</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific OWL class in OE9, modeled as a subclass of PhysicalFeature. JointFeature, MultiPartStructureFeature, and RotationalStructureFeature are its subclasses. AFFORDANCE20Q is a background reference rather than the source of this exact OWL definition. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): This source supports treating physical characteristics as evidence for affordance reasoning. Team Affordance defines StructuralFeature locally as: &quot;physical feature describing the organization, arrangement, or relationship of parts within a physical entity&quot;. The publication is conceptual motivation, not a formal OWL class definition. Dictionary entry supplies only the ordinary meaning of the physical characteristic. AFFORDANCE20Q is retained as background motivation, not as the source of this exact OWL class definition.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A configuration involving two blade parts connected through a rotational joint may be described using structural features when those characteristics are explicitly represented.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use StructuralFeature for represented structural characteristics of physical entities. Use GeometricFeature for local shape or dimensional characteristics, and ObjectPart with hasPart for explicitly represented constituent parts.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Structural Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Covers structural physical features describing the organization, arrangement, or relationships of parts within a physical entity, rather than local geometric, material, mechanical, or surface characteristics.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">In OE9, StructuralFeature is a subclass of PhysicalFeature. JointFeature concerns connections between parts, MultiPartStructureFeature concerns arrangements of multiple parts, and RotationalStructureFeature concerns relative rotational movement between parts.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/StructuralRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">structural requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” Findings of ACL: EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">action requirement concerning part structure or relationships between parts needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Adapted from PART scoping and structural descriptions. Constraints on arrangement and connection extend KARI’s grammar. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Parent aligned with Diagram 1. Requirement class created by Team Affordance to specify a condition over a modeled feature; AFFORDANCE20Q motivates physical-property evidence but does not define this OWL class. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A paired-blade CUT pathway requires two relevant parts connected in the represented arrangement.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Compare supplied facts with the represented requirement. Explicit conflict and missing information are distinct outcomes.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Structural Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-general requirement category. Its specific constraints depend on the action and represented physical conditions. Target-conditioned required levels may be expressed through RequirementMapping when explicitly modeled; no fixed threshold is implied by this class alone.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">A structural requirement concerns the entity’s organization. An interaction requirement concerns how entities interact.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SurfaceFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">surface feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Jiang, Y., Yang, M., Li, Z., &amp; Pujara, J. (2026). AFFORDANCE20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties. arXiv:2606.14240v2. A. Myers, C. L. Teo, C. Fermüller and Y. Aloimonos, &quot;Affordance detection of tool parts from geometric features,&quot; 2015 IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA), Seattle, WA, USA, 2015, pp. 1374-1381, doi: 10.1109/ICRA.2015.7139369. keywords: {Robots;Feature extraction;Shape;Image segmentation;Three-dimensional displays;Geometry;Conferences}, Cambridge Dictionary, “Surface.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/surface</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Surface.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/surface</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>a characteristic of the outer boundary of a physical entity, such as friction or smoothness, that may affect contact with another entity</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Earlier R comment moved from source field: Affordance20Q supports physical-property-grounded affordance reasoning but does not define the SurfaceFeature ontology class. SurfaceFeature is locally authored; the publication serves as conceptual background only. The dictionary establishes only the general meaning of a surface; SurfaceFeature is a locally defined OWL class. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">For example, a blade surface can be characterized by a smoothness feature represented with a categorical feature level.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use as the parent category for outer-surface properties.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Surface Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">SURFACE is one of four allowed physical-property dimensions.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">The benchmark treats surface as distinct from material, shape, and size.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SurfaceRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">surface requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, “Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,” Findings of ACL: EMNLP, 2026. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/ Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">action requirement concerning a surface characteristic needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Adapted from SURFACE predicates and surface-texture questions. The requirement class is project-specific. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Parent aligned with Diagram 1. Requirement class created by Team Affordance to specify a condition over a modeled feature; AFFORDANCE20Q motivates physical-property evidence but does not define this OWL class. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A modeled contact pathway specifies a surface-texture condition where evidence supports it.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Compare supplied facts with the represented requirement. Explicit conflict and missing information are distinct outcomes.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Surface Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-general requirement category. Its specific constraints depend on the action and represented physical conditions. Target-conditioned required levels may be expressed through RequirementMapping when explicitly modeled; no fixed threshold is implied by this class alone.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Surface requirements may concern texture or a separately supplied friction property.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/TensileStrengthFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/StrengthFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">tensile strength feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">D. Roylance, &apos;Introduction to Elastic Response,&apos; Mechanics of Materials lecture notes, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, January 21, 2000. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-11-mechanics-of-materials-fall-1999/resources/mit3_11f99_elas_1/ Y. Jiang, M. Yang, Z. Li, and J. Pujara, &apos;Affordance20Q: Evaluating Affordance Reasoning from Physical Properties,&apos; Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2026, pp. 35930–35948. https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1683/</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">D. Roylance, &apos;Introduction to Elastic Response,&apos; Mechanics of Materials lecture notes, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, January 21, 2000. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-11-mechanics-of-materials-fall-1999/resources/mit3_11f99_elas_1/</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">strength feature describing resistance to failure when subjected to tensile force</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific working definition. The paper does not define tensile strength. The label follows TensileStrengthProperty in the conceptual model. Working definition adapted for the Week 5 conceptual model; not a verbatim source definition. Corrected Tensil to Tensile. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from Roylance&apos;s treatment of tensile strength in a material tension test. TensileStrengthFeature is a project-specific qualitative class rather than an exact numerical test result.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">A candidate part has a supplied tensile-strength value with a stated test or failure criterion.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Attach the known property to its actual physical bearer; use it only through evidence-backed requirement relationships.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Tensile Strength Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-independent property category. Known candidate-side values are supplied as input; object labels are not reasoning evidence.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Tensile strength concerns failure under pulling loads. It does not imply rigidity or suitability for every CUT pathway.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/TensileStrengthRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/StrengthRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">tensile strength requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">strength requirement concerning resistance to tensile force needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate tensile strength value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning tensile strength; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Tensile Strength Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to strength requirements concerning resistance to tensile force.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">TensileStrengthRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ThicknessFeature">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SizeFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">thickness feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">B. Barati and E. Karana, “Affordances as Materials Potential: What Design Can Do for Materials Development,” International Journal of Design, vol. 13, no. 3, pp. 105–123, 2019. OBO Foundry, “Phenotype and Trait Ontology (PATO): Thickness (PATO:0000915),” [Online]. Available: https://obofoundry.org/ontology/pato.html.</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">Phenotype and Trait Ontology (PATO), “thickness” (PATO:0000915), OBO Foundry.</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>a size-related geometric characteristic describing the separation between opposing surfaces of a physical entity or its constituent component</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Round 2 revision distinguishes general Thickness from specialized local edge geometry such as Thin Edge or Thick Edge. Thickness describes a dimensional extent and does not by itself imply a particular cutting-edge geometry or action-feasibility role. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Project-specific wording adapted from PATO&apos;s definition of Thickness as a one-dimensional extent through an object, distinguished from length and width; terminology has been generalized from PATO&apos;s quality framework to the project&apos;s physical-property vocabulary. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT example: the overall thickness of a plastic sheet or card may be represented as Thickness, while the local thickness of its working edge is represented separately when edge geometry is relevant to the cutting configuration</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Used to represent a known one-dimensional extent of an Object or Object Part when that dimension is relevant to an Action Requirement. Thickness alone does not establish action feasibility and is not inherently an Enabling Property or Blocking Property</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Thickness Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-independent geometric property. Thickness may characterize an Object or Object Part and may be relevant to different Action Requirements depending on the action and physical configuration. General Thickness should be distinguished from specialized local measurements such as the thickness of a cutting edge</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Thickness is a dimensional characteristic of an entity and should be distinguished from Material Property, Shape, and local edge-condition concepts. The Thickness of an Object or Object Part does not by itself specify the geometry of a working edge; edge-local thickness may require a more specific representation.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ThicknessRequirement">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/SizeRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">thickness requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">size requirement concerning the thickness needed to support an action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific ontology class represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Generic meaning of a necessary condition; specialized property type and CUT criteria are ontology-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Illustrative requirement: a modeled action might require an appropriate thickness value of a relevant part or target.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class to classify applicable action requirements concerning thickness; link a concrete requirement to its feature via concernsFeature.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Thickness Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to size requirements concerning thickness.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">ThicknessRequirement is a Team Affordance subclass used to organize physical constraints. Satisfaction or conflict must be evaluated in the context of an Assessment and explicit feature evidence.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/TwoCUTRequirementsSatisfiedAssessment">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Assessment"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">two cut requirements satisfied assessment</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">assessment in which both modeled cutting requirements are represented as satisfied</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Team-defined Assessment subclass for the two modeled CUT requirements (rigidity and edge thickness). This is a controlled regression-test classification, not a general feasibility guarantee for arbitrary targets or actions. Lexical basis only: Supports the underlying requirement concept; exactly which two CUT requirements count as satisfied is local. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">testAAssessment represents a controlled case with two satisfied CUT requirements.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use this class when the stated structural or assessment restrictions are supported by explicitly modeled assertions.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Two CUT Requirements Satisfied Assessment</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to the two modeled CUT requirements in this test setting; does not by itself establish universal feasibility across configurations.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">This class pertains only to the current two modeled CUT requirements. Future extensions to additional requirements must not inherit feasibility conclusions automatically.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<owl:Class rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/WholeObject">
		<rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalEntity"/>
		<rdfs:subClassOf>
			<owl:Restriction>
				<owl:onProperty rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasPart"/>
				<owl:allValuesFrom rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ObjectPart"/>
			</owl:Restriction>
		</rdfs:subClassOf>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">whole object</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">Basic Formal Ontology (BFO), &quot;object&quot; (BFO:0000030). http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000030</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:contributor xml:lang="en">LZ</dcterms:contributor>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">Basic Formal Ontology (BFO), &quot;object&quot; (BFO:0000030). http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000030</dcterms:source>
		<skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Physical Object</skos:altLabel>
		<skos:definition>a complete material object treated as a single unit rather than as one of its constituent components, which may serve as a candidate or target in an assessment</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Inspired by BFO&apos;s representation of objects as material entities. WholeObject is a project-specific class representing a physical entity as a whole rather than as one of its constituent parts. It supports candidate and target descriptions in action feasibility assessments and is not formally equated with BFO Object. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">In the CUT test cases, cardCandidate and carrotTarget are instances of WholeObject. The card candidate has represented edge thickness and rigidity values, while the carrot target has a represented hardness value. These object names are internal references, not feasibility evidence.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use WholeObject to represent a complete candidate or target object, and ObjectPart to represent its constituent or working parts. Evaluate CUT feasibility using explicitly represented physical features, relevant part relationships, and action requirements rather than object names or conventional functions.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Whole Object</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Represents a whole physical entity that may serve as a candidate or target in an action assessment. WholeObject is a subclass of PhysicalEntity and is disjoint with ObjectPart. Classification as a WholeObject does not itself establish CUT feasibility.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">A whole object may be described through physical feature values using hasFeatureValue and through constituent parts using hasPart. In the ontology, the hasPart restriction for WholeObject specifies that its parts are ObjectPart instances; it does not require every whole object to have an explicitly represented part.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:Class>
	
	<swrl:Variable rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/a">
	</swrl:Variable>
	
	<swrl:Variable rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/act">
	</swrl:Variable>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/affectsRequirement">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">affects requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/RequirementMapping"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates a requirement mapping to the action requirement whose required level is determined by the mapping</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Supports the requirement terminology; the affectsRequirement property direction and semantics are local. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">cutRigidityMapping affectsRequirement cutRigidityRequirement.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Requirement Mapping to Action Requirement to identify the requirement whose required level is specified by the mapping. This relation does not by itself state whether the requirement is satisfied.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Affects Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to linking a Requirement Mapping to the Action Requirement whose required feature level is determined by that mapping.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">affectsRequirement represents an explicit relationship in the physical-property reasoning graph, not a default relation inferred from a term’s spelling.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
