	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/concernsFeature">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">concerns feature</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalFeature"/>
		<skos:definition>links an action requirement to the type of physical characteristic used to evaluate whether that requirement is met</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property in OE9 relating ActionRequirement to PhysicalFeature. It identifies the feature addressed by a requirement without determining whether the requirement is satisfied. Lexical basis only: Supports the feature terminology; concernsFeature is a locally designed object property. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT example: cutRigidityRequirement concernsFeature rigidity.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from ActionRequirement to PhysicalFeature. Requirement satisfaction or conflict must be represented separately through the assessment.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Concerns Feature</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Domain: ActionRequirement; range: PhysicalFeature. Identifies the physical feature concerned by an action requirement.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">The relationship identifies what feature is required, not whether the evaluated candidate meets the requirement.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/conflictsWithRequirement">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">conflicts with requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Assessment"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement"/>
		<owl:propertyDisjointWith rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasUnresolvedRequirement"/>
		<owl:propertyDisjointWith rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/satisfiesRequirement"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates an assessment to an action requirement that is not satisfied by the evaluated candidate configuration</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property in OE9 relating Assessment to ActionRequirement. It records a conflict between an evaluated candidate configuration and a modeled action requirement. Lexical basis only: Supports the requirement terminology; conflict determination is formalized by local rules. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT example: the sponge-carrot assessment conflicts with the modeled rigidity requirement when the candidate has insufficient rigidity.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Assessment to ActionRequirement when the evaluated candidate configuration conflicts with the requirement. Do not infer conflict merely because satisfiesRequirement is absent.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Conflicts With Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Domain: Assessment; range: ActionRequirement. Represents a requirement conflict established from the represented physical conditions; absence of a satisfaction assertion does not itself establish a conflict.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">A conflict with a modeled CUT requirement can support an infeasible judgment under the implemented reasoning rules.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/cut">
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Action"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">cut</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>M. A. Moreno-Mateos and P. Steinmann, “Cutting soft materials: how material differences shape the response,” npj Computational Materials, vol. 12, art. no. 15, 2026, doi: 10.1038/s41524-025-01869-y Cambridge Dictionary, “Cut.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/cut</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Cut,” verb (USE KNIFE). https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/cut</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>to break the surface of something, or to divide or make something smaller, using a sharp tool, especially a knife</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>This definition is quoted verbatim from the indicated Cambridge Dictionary sense. The individual is a locally named CUT action. The scope and explanatory notes describe the ontology-specific CUT modeling assumptions; those assumptions are not part of the dictionary definition.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT example: a rigid plastic card may support CUT of a carrot when the card edge and target conditions satisfy the applicable modeled requirements, even though the card is not conventionally categorized as a cutting tool.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Used as the Action whose applicable Action Requirements are retrieved and evaluated against the known Physical Properties, ObjectParts, Target, and Physical Conditions of a specified action attempt. Feasibility is inferred from represented property–requirement relationships rather than from conventional object names or functions</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Cut</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Action-specific concept but not mechanism-specific. The current course implementation focuses on CUT in everyday kitchen contexts, but CUT is not restricted to a particular tool, Object Part, edge geometry, or cutting mechanism. Different physical configurations may realize the action under different requirements and conditions</skos:scopeNote>
		<hasRequirement rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/cutEdgeThicknessRequirement"/>
		<hasRequirement rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/cutRigidityRequirement"/>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">CUT may involve different combinations of indentation, fracture, shear, contact, and relative motion depending on the target material and physical configuration. Complete division of the target into separate pieces is not required; a localized incision or propagated cut may also instantiate the action. The ontology therefore represents requirements for particular cutting configurations rather than treating one property set as the universal definition of CUT.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/cutEdgeThicknessMapping">
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/RequirementMapping"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">cut edge thickness mapping</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Mapping.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/mapping</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Mapping.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/mapping</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">requirement mapping that maps high target hardness to a low required edge thickness level for the cut edge thickness requirement</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific named individual introduced in the RDF/OWL ontology to instantiate controlled CUT competency-question test cases; not adopted from an external terminology source. This named ABox individual is locally constructed test data, not an externally documented instance. The R-column reference supports only the general lexical meaning of the indicated word; it does not substantiate the individual&apos;s particular physical feature assertions, ordinal levels, or expected reasoning result. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Cut Edge Thickness Mapping</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Applies to the controlled CUT feasibility test cases represented in the current course ontology.</skos:scopeNote>
		<affectsRequirement rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/cutEdgeThicknessRequirement"/>
		<specifiesRequiredLevel rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/low"/>
		<usesTargetFeatureType rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hardness"/>
		<usesTargetLevel rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/high"/>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/cutEdgeThicknessRequirement">
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ThicknessRequirement"/>
