	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/edgeThickness">
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ThicknessFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">edge thickness</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Thickness.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/us/dictionary/english/thickness</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Thickness.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/us/dictionary/english/thickness</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>a dimensional characteristic of a working edge, describing the distance between its opposing surfaces near the contact region</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific named individual introduced in the RDF/OWL ontology to instantiate controlled CUT competency-question test cases; not adopted from an external terminology source. This named ABox individual is locally constructed test data, not an externally documented instance. The R-column reference supports only the general lexical meaning of the indicated word; it does not substantiate the individual&apos;s particular physical feature assertions, ordinal levels, or expected reasoning result. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Edge Thickness</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Edge thickness represents the thickness of a relevant working edge. Low indicates a relatively thin edge, Medium indicates intermediate thickness, and High indicates a relatively thick edge within the controlled test context. For the currently modeled high-hardness target, Low edge thickness is the required level. Edge thickness is distinct from overall object thickness, blade wedge angle, and sharpness.</skos:scopeNote>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	
	<swrl:Variable rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/feature">
	</swrl:Variable>
	
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/flexibility">
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FlexibilityFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">flexibility</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Flexibility.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/flexibility</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Flexibility,” noun (ABLE TO BEND). https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/flexibility</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>the ability to bend or to be bent easily without breaking</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>This physical sense is quoted verbatim from the indicated Cambridge Dictionary entry. The named ontology individual denotes the feature type; its usage in CUT assessments is specified separately and is not an externally sourced individual.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Flexibility</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Applies to the controlled CUT feasibility test cases represented in the current course ontology.</skos:scopeNote>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hardness">
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/HardnessFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">hardness</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Hardness.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/hardness</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Hardness.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/hardness</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">physical feature representing the hardness of a physical entity</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific named individual introduced in the RDF/OWL ontology to instantiate controlled CUT competency-question test cases; not adopted from an external terminology source. This named ABox individual is locally constructed test data, not an externally documented instance. The R-column reference supports only the general lexical meaning of the indicated word; it does not substantiate the individual&apos;s particular physical feature assertions, ordinal levels, or expected reasoning result. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Hardness</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Hardness represents resistance to localized deformation or indentation. Low, Medium, and High describe increasing qualitative levels of target hardness within the controlled evaluation context. The current CUT requirement mappings are defined for High-hardness targets, such as the represented carrot example. These levels are not calibrated numerical hardness measurements and should not be interpreted as direct measurements of cutting force.</skos:scopeNote>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasAction">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has action</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Assessment"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Action"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates an assessment to the action being evaluated</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Supports assessment context; the hasAction predicate is locally modeled. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">testAAssessment hasAction cut.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Assessment to Action to identify the action whose feasibility is being evaluated.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Has Action</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to identifying the Action evaluated within an Assessment.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">The action is what is evaluated in the assessment. The same physical entity can be assessed for different actions.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasCandidate">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has candidate</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Assessment"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalEntity"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates an assessment to the physical entity being evaluated as the candidate for performing the action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Supports assessment context; the hasCandidate predicate is locally modeled. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">testAAssessment hasCandidate testCandidateA.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Assessment to Physical Entity to identify the candidate being evaluated. The candidate label or conventional object function is not reasoning evidence.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Has Candidate</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to identifying the Physical Entity evaluated as the candidate within an Assessment.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">The candidate is a role in an assessment, not an intrinsic named category or evidence of the action’s feasibility.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasFeatureType">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has feature type</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">W3C, Semantic Sensor Network Ontology (SOSA/SSN), W3C Recommendation, 2017. https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-ssn/</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>W3C, Semantic Sensor Network Ontology (SOSA/SSN), W3C Recommendation, 2017. https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-ssn/</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureValue"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalFeature"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates a feature value to the physical feature type that it describes</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Prior non-bibliographic R note: Conceptual parallel to SOSA/SSN observedProperty (identifying the property described); hasFeatureType is a Team Affordance property connecting FeatureValue to PhysicalFeature, not a replacement for sosa:observedProperty. SOSA/SSN does not define the local property; only the general separation of observable property, feature and result is borrowed. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">testARigidityValue hasFeatureType rigidity.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Feature Value to Physical Feature to identify which physical feature the value represents.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Has Feature Type</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to identifying the Physical Feature type represented by a Feature Value.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">hasFeatureType represents an explicit relationship in the physical-property reasoning graph, not a default relation inferred from a term’s spelling.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasFeatureValue">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has feature value</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">W3C, Semantic Sensor Network Ontology (SOSA/SSN), W3C Recommendation, 2017. https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-ssn/</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>W3C, Semantic Sensor Network Ontology (SOSA/SSN), W3C Recommendation, 2017. https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-ssn/</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalEntity"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureValue"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates a physical entity to a feature value that specifies one of its physical features and its level</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Prior non-bibliographic R note: Conceptually informed by SOSA/SSN representation of an entity, its observable properties, and a result. hasFeatureValue links PhysicalEntity to a reified qualitative FeatureValue; it is not equivalent to sosa:hasResult or sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest. SOSA/SSN does not define the local property; only the general separation of observable property, feature and result is borrowed. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">testCandidateA hasFeatureValue testARigidityValue.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Physical Entity to Feature Value to associate explicitly represented physical feature information with its bearer. Do not infer feature values solely from an object name or conventional function.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Has Feature Value</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to associating a Physical Entity with a Feature Value that represents one of its physical features and categorical levels.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Feature values are attached to the actual bearer (whole object or relevant object part); do not infer physical values from an object label.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasJudgment">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has judgment</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Assessment"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeasibilityJudgment"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates an assessment to its feasibility judgment</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Supports assessment context; the hasJudgment predicate is locally modeled. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">Expected inference: testAAssessment hasJudgment feasibleJudgment.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Assessment to Feasibility Judgment to associate an assessment with its outcome. When testing inferred feasibility, do not use this relation to pre-assert the answer being evaluated.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Has Judgment</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to associating an Assessment with a Feasibility Judgment.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Connects an assessment to its final feasibility judgment. A cardinality axiom alone does not materialize a named judgment or implement priority between conflicting outcomes.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasLevel">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has level</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">W3C, Semantic Sensor Network Ontology (SOSA/SSN), W3C Recommendation, 2017. https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-ssn/</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>W3C, Semantic Sensor Network Ontology (SOSA/SSN), W3C Recommendation, 2017. https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-ssn/</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureValue"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureLevel"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates a feature value to its categorical feature level</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Prior non-bibliographic R note: Conceptually informed by SOSA/SSN separation of a property and its observation result. hasLevel associates a project-specific FeatureValue with a categorical FeatureLevel; SOSA/SSN does not define this qualitative level relation. SOSA/SSN does not define the local property; only the general separation of observable property, feature and result is borrowed. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">testARigidityValue hasLevel high.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Feature Value to Feature Level to identify its represented categorical level, such as low, medium, or high.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Has Level</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to associating a Feature Value with its categorical Feature Level.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">The qualitative level is attached to a FeatureValue and should be interpreted according to the feature and context.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasPart">
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="&owl;AsymmetricProperty"/>
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="&owl;IrreflexiveProperty"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has part</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">OBO Relations Ontology (RO), BFO:0000051 (&quot;has part&quot;), http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:contributor xml:lang="en">LZ</dcterms:contributor>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">OBO Relations Ontology (RO), BFO:0000051 (&quot;has part&quot;), http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalEntity"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ObjectPart"/>
		<owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/isPartOf"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates a physical entity to an object part that constitutes part of that entity</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from the general has-part relation in the OBO Relations Ontology. The project-specific property connects a PhysicalEntity to an ObjectPart and is used to represent structural relationships relevant to action feasibility.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">knifeCandidate hasPart knifeBlade.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use to connect a whole entity or object to one of its component parts.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Has Part</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">A structural relation connecting an entity to a constituent part.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">The ARCLIGHT representation uses a has-part relation as part of its entity representation.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasRequirement">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Action"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates an action to an action requirement relevant to evaluating its physical feasibility</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Team-defined relationship introduced in the OE_5 conceptual model, linking an Action to a modeled ActionRequirement. The prior model documents its internal development history, not an independent external definition or standard relation. Lexical basis only: Supports requirement terminology; hasRequirement is a local object property. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT example: CUT has a requirement for suitable working-edge geometry within a modeled edge-based cutting method.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Action to ActionRequirement to associate an action with its represented requirements. Represent any joint or alternative requirement structure separately rather than assuming that every linked requirement is universally necessary.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Has Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Associates an action with a physical feasibility requirement within the modeled scope. The current implementation focuses on CUT. The relation does not assert that a candidate satisfies the requirement.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">An action may have multiple requirements involving physical properties, part structure, or interactions. The relation alone does not specify whether linked requirements must be satisfied jointly or belong to alternative ways of performing the action.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasTarget">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has target</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Assessment.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/assessment</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Assessment"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalEntity"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates an assessment to the physical entity that serves as the target of the action</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Supports assessment context; hasTarget is a local object property. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">testAAssessment hasTarget carrotTarget.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Assessment to Physical Entity to identify the target of the evaluated action. The target label or conventional object function is not itself reasoning evidence.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Has Target</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to identifying the Physical Entity serving as the target within an Assessment.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Target is a context-dependent assessment role; its explicitly represented properties can determine applicable action requirements.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasUnresolvedRequirement">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has unresolved requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Assessment"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement"/>
		<owl:propertyDisjointWith rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/satisfiesRequirement"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">Relates an assessment to an action requirement whose satisfaction or conflict cannot be determined from the explicitly represented information.</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Team-defined property added in OE_11. An unresolved requirement is explicitly identified from an unspecified feature; RDF absence alone is not evidence of &quot;unknown&quot;. This is separate from conflicting requirements and does not by itself resolve all competing final judgments. Lexical basis only: Supports the requirement terminology; unresolved-status conditions and disjointness are defined locally. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">testCAssessment hasUnresolvedRequirement cutEdgeThicknessRequirement.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Assessment to ActionRequirement when the relevant feature information is explicitly marked as unspecified. Do not treat missing RDF assertions alone as evidence of an unresolved requirement.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Has Unresolved Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Domain: Assessment; range: ActionRequirement. Applies to requirements whose status cannot be determined from explicitly represented physical-feature information.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Explicitly relates an assessment to a requirement that is unresolved, distinct from both satisfaction and conflict. It cannot be inferred merely because no requirement assertion exists.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/hasUnspecifiedFeatureType">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">has unspecified feature type</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Feature.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/feature</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalEntity"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalFeature"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relationship indicating that the value of a physical feature type has not been supplied for a physical entity in the current assessment</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property added in OE9 to explicitly record an unspecified feature type; this is not a general closed-world inference from missing OWL assertions. Lexical basis only: Supports feature terminology; explicit unspecified markers and open-world handling are local semantics. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT Test C: testCandidateC hasUnspecifiedFeatureType edgeThickness.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Physical Entity to Physical Feature to identify a feature type whose value is unspecified in the current assessment.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Has Unspecified Feature Type</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Domain: Physical Entity; range: Physical Feature. Applies when the assessment explicitly records that a feature value has not been supplied.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Marks a feature type as explicitly unspecified for an entity; absence of a hasFeatureValue assertion is not automatically an unspecified feature under OWL open-world semantics.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/high">
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureLevel"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">high</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “High.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/high</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “High,” adjective (ABOVE AVERAGE). https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/high</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>the upper category in a three-level qualitative scale describing the relative degree of a specified physical feature under the modeled conditions</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Definition adapted from the Cambridge Dictionary sense of &apos;high&apos;. The individual represents the upper qualitative FeatureLevel category for a specified physical feature, not a universal numerical measurement or a guarantee of higher action feasibility.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:note>Usage: Represents a relatively high degree of a physical feature or property. This individual may be assigned as a level value across different feature classes through the hasLevel property. Its interpretation depends on the specific feature and evaluation context.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">High</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">The upper qualitative level of a physical feature within a specified context. Its physical interpretation depends on the feature type; a High level does not necessarily imply greater action feasibility.</skos:scopeNote>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/interactsWith">
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="&owl;IrreflexiveProperty"/>
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="&owl;SymmetricProperty"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">interacts with</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Interact.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/interact</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:contributor xml:lang="en">LZ</dcterms:contributor>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Interact.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/interact</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ObjectPart"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ObjectPart"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates an object part to another object part with which it physically interacts in a configuration</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Team-defined relation for explicitly represented physical interaction between object parts in an action configuration. This is not equivalent to the general part-of relation; an interaction is not inferred merely because two parts exist. Lexical basis only: Supports the meaning of physical interaction; interactsWith is locally modeled for ObjectPart. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT example: one blade part of a pair of scissors interacts with the other blade part as their cutting edges move past one another during cutting.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from ObjectPart to ObjectPart when the supplied description represents a physical interaction relevant to the action. Do not infer interaction solely from shared object membership, proximity, or an object name.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Interacts With</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Represents physical interaction between Object Parts relevant to the modeled action. The relation does not by itself establish requirement satisfaction or action feasibility.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Part interaction provides relational information beyond the physical properties of each part considered separately. Two parts belonging to the same object does not by itself establish that they interact.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/isPartOf">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">is part of</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation xml:lang="en">OBO Relations Ontology (RO), BFO:0000050 (&quot;part of&quot;), http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:contributor xml:lang="en">LZ</dcterms:contributor>
		<dcterms:source xml:lang="en">OBO Relations Ontology (RO), BFO:0000050 (&quot;part of&quot;), http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000050</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ObjectPart"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/PhysicalEntity"/>
		<skos:definition>links a constituent component to the larger physical entity to which it belongs</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property represented in OE9. Defined as the inverse of hasPart, expressing the part-to-entity direction of the relationship. Earlier adaptation/context note (not a source citation): Adapted from the general part-of relation in the OBO Relations Ontology. The project-specific property relates an ObjectPart to its containing PhysicalEntity and is modeled as the inverse of hasPart. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">An object part may be related to its containing physical entity through isPartOf.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Object Part to the containing Physical Entity. The inverse direction is represented by hasPart.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Is Part Of</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Domain: Object Part; range: Physical Entity. Represents structural membership rather than action feasibility.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">Inverse structural relation corresponding to hasPart. An object part can carry feature values relevant to the feasibility assessment.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/low">
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureLevel"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">low</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Low.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/low</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Low,” adjective (LEVEL). https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/low</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>the lower category in a three-level qualitative scale describing the relative degree of a specified physical feature under the modeled conditions</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Definition adapted from the Cambridge Dictionary sense of &apos;low&apos;. The individual represents the lower qualitative FeatureLevel category for a specified physical feature; whether that level satisfies a CUT requirement depends on the feature and the assessment context.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:note>Usage: Represents a relatively low degree of a physical feature or property. This individual may be assigned as a level value across different feature classes through the hasLevel property. Its interpretation depends on the specific feature and evaluation context.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Low</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">The lower qualitative level of a physical feature within a specified context. Its physical interpretation depends on the feature type; for example, Low edge thickness may satisfy a CUT requirement, whereas Low rigidity may conflict with another.</skos:scopeNote>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	
	<swrl:Variable rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/m">
	</swrl:Variable>
	
	<swrl:Variable rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/mE">
	</swrl:Variable>
	
	<swrl:Variable rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/mR">
	</swrl:Variable>
	
	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/medium">
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureLevel"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">medium</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Medium.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/medium</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Medium,” adjective (MIDDLE). https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/medium</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>the intermediate category in a three-level qualitative scale describing the relative degree of a specified physical feature under the modeled conditions</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Definition adapted from the Cambridge Dictionary sense of &apos;medium&apos;. The individual represents the intermediate qualitative FeatureLevel category, without assuming equally spaced numeric levels or a universal measurement threshold.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:note>Usage: Represents an intermediate degree of a physical feature or property. This individual may be assigned as a level value across different feature classes through the hasLevel property. Its interpretation depends on the specific feature and evaluation context.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Medium</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">The intermediate qualitative level between Low and High for a physical feature. It is available in the ontology but is not fully covered by the current CUT conflict and feasibility rules.</skos:scopeNote>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	
