	<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/rigidity">
		<rdf:type rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/RigidityFeature"/>
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">rigidity</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Rigidity.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/rigidity</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Rigidity,” noun (STIFFNESS). https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/rigidity</dcterms:source>
		<skos:definition>the quality of being stiff, fixed, or impossible to bend</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>This physical sense is quoted verbatim from Cambridge Dictionary. The named ontology individual denotes a physical feature type; its qualitative degree in a particular assessment is represented separately with FeatureValue and FeatureLevel.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Rigidity</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Rigidity represents resistance to bending or deformation under an applied load. In the controlled CUT scenarios, Low indicates that the entity deforms relatively easily, Medium indicates intermediate resistance, and High indicates sufficient resistance to maintain its relevant working geometry under the assumed cutting conditions. These categories are qualitative and context-dependent, not defined by universal numerical thresholds.</skos:scopeNote>
	</owl:NamedIndividual>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/satisfiesRequirement">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">satisfies requirement</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/Assessment"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/ActionRequirement"/>
		<skos:definition>links an assessment to an action requirement when the represented physical feature information meets the modeled condition for that requirement</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property in OE9 relating Assessment to ActionRequirement. It records that the evaluated candidate configuration satisfies a modeled action requirement. Lexical basis only: Supports the requirement terminology; satisfaction determination is formalized by local rules. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">CUT example: the card-carrot assessment satisfies the modeled rigidity and edge thickness requirements in the controlled test.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Assessment to ActionRequirement when the evaluated candidate configuration meets the specified requirement under the represented target conditions.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Satisfies Requirement</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Domain: Assessment; range: ActionRequirement. Represents satisfaction of a particular requirement; satisfaction of one requirement alone does not establish overall CUT feasibility.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">An assessment may satisfy one CUT requirement while another remains conflicting or unspecified.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
	<owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/specifiesRequiredLevel">
		<rdfs:label xml:lang="en">specifies required level</rdfs:label>
		<dcterms:bibliographicCitation>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:bibliographicCitation>
		<dcterms:source>Cambridge Dictionary, “Requirement.” https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/requirement</dcterms:source>
		<rdfs:domain rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/RequirementMapping"/>
		<rdfs:range rdf:resource="https://tw.rpi.edu/ontology-engineering/oe2026/physical-properties/FeatureLevel"/>
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en">relates a requirement mapping to the feature level required for the affected action requirement</skos:definition>
		<skos:editorialNote>Project-specific object property represented in the current RDF/OWL implementation; terminology entry added to align the curated term list with the implemented ontology. Lexical basis only: Supports requirement terminology; target-dependent level selection is project-specific. The dictionary does not define this specific OWL class or property. Concept/term developed for the current ontology; internal artifact history is provenance, not an external literature source. Preferred label is locally assigned for this ontology; no claim of external publication as the label origin.</skos:editorialNote>
		<skos:example xml:lang="en">cutRigidityMapping specifiesRequiredLevel high.</skos:example>
		<skos:note xml:lang="en">Usage: Use from Requirement Mapping to Feature Level to specify the level required for the affected Action Requirement.</skos:note>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Specifies Required Level</skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Restricted to identifying the Feature Level required by the Action Requirement affected by a Requirement Mapping.</skos:scopeNote>
		<cmns-av:explanatoryNote xml:lang="en">specifiesRequiredLevel represents an explicit relationship in the physical-property reasoning graph, not a default relation inferred from a term’s spelling.</cmns-av:explanatoryNote>
	</owl:ObjectProperty>
	
